%N A058914 Continued fraction expansion of the Minkowski-Bower constant.
%C A058914 Continued fraction expansions for the other fixed points (mod 1) are {0}, {0,2} and A120221. - Joseph Biberstine (jrbibers(AT)indiana.edu), Jun 10 2006
%C A058914 It is conjectured that Minkowski's question mark function has exactly five fixed points on [0,1], or four modulo 1; see Gayfulin and Shulga, Conjecture 1. - _Eric W. Weisstein_, Oct 08 2026
%D A058914 J. H. Conway, On Numbers and Games, Academic Press, New-York, 1976.
%H A058914 Nikita Shulga, <a href="/A058914/b058914.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..5489</a>
%H A058914 Dmitry Gayfulin and Nikita Shulga, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.10139">Diophantine properties of fixed points of Minkowski question mark function</a>, arXiv:1811.10139 [math.NT], 2018.
%H A058914 Nikita Shulga, <a href="/A058914/a058914.txt">5000+ terms</a>
%H A058914 Nikita A. Shulga, <a href="https://www.moebiuscontest.ru/files/2020/shulga.pdf">Diophantine properties of fixed points and derivative of iterations of Minkowski question mark function</a>, Lomonosov Moscow State University, Möbius Contest 2020.
%H A058914 <a href="/index/Me#MinkowskiQ">Index entries for sequences related to Minkowski's question mark function</a>
%t A058914 ContinuedFraction[x /. FindRoot[MinkowskiQuestionMark[x] == x, {x, 42/100, 421/1000}, WorkingPrecision -> 100, MaxIterations -> 500], 20] (* _Eric W. Weisstein_, Oct 08 2026 *)
